api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/downloads","issues_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/issues{/number}","pulls_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/pulls{/number}","milestones_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/milestones{/number}","notifications_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/notifications{?since,all,participating}","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/labels{/name}","releases_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/releases{/id}","deployments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/deployments","created_at":"2008-04-11T02:19:47Z","updated_at":"2025-12-13T07:43:35Z","pushed_at":"2025-12-13T03:40:43Z","git_url":"git://github.com/rails/rails.git","ssh_url":"git@github.com:rails/rails.git","clone_url":"https://github.com/rails/rails.git","svn_url":"https://github.com/rails/rails","homepage":"https://rubyonrails.org","size":275235,"stargazers_count":57965,"watchers_count":57965,"language":"Ruby","has_issues":true,"has_projects":true,"has_downloads":true,"has_wiki":false,"has_pages":false,"has_discussions":true,"forks_count":22063,"mirror_url":null,"archived":false,"disabled":false,"open_issues_count":1385,"license":{"key":"mit","name":"MIT License","spdx_id":"MIT","url":"https://api.github.com/licenses/mit","node_id":"MDc6TGljZW5zZTEz"},"allow_forking":true,"is_template":false,"web_commit_signoff_required":false,"topics":["activejob","activerecord","framework","html","mvc","rails","ruby"],"visibility":"public","forks":22063,"open_issues":1385,"watchers":57965,"default_branch":"main","permissions":{"admin":false,"maintain":false,"push":false,"triage":false,"pull":true}},"body":"I've been working on converting [our application](https://github.com/openstreetmap/openstreetmap-website) to use [multiple databases](https://guides.rubyonrails.org/active_record_multiple_databases.html) in order to read from replicas. I have found a problem where the default `:writing` connection can be erroneously marked as readonly, and this causes problems with our test suite. I've managed to isolate the problematic behaviour into a small demo application (one controller, one test, standard DatabaseSelector middleware).\r\n\r\n### Steps to reproduce\r\n\r\nDemo application to show problem: https://github.com/gravitystorm/prevent_writes\r\n\r\nDiff between \"rails new\" and demo application: https://github.com/gravitystorm/prevent_writes/compare/3e2defa..master\r\n\r\nThe isolated [testcase](https://github.com/gravitystorm/prevent_writes/blob/d28877247e36de3aee5ad6c28793e487d58c234c/test/controllers/site_controller_test.rb) is essentially:\r\n* `get` a page\r\n* then try writing to the database outside of the read request\r\n* write connection has been marked as readonly and the testcase fails.\r\n\r\nIt's worth noting the following things:\r\n\r\n* The problem only happens if responding to the `get` involves a database connection being used. If you remove the `c = User.count` from the controller action, the problem does not happen.\r\n* The problem can be avoided by accessing the database within the test, before the `get` request. In the demo testcase, if you uncomment the section `# user = User.count` before the `get`, the testcase then passes. This shows that even using the default connection to make a read request is enough to avoid it being later marked as read-only.\r\n* If there are any fixtures files, even if they are blank, then the problem does not happen. I think this is another case of using the database connection for something else first means that we avoid the problem.\r\n\r\nUnfortunately I got completely lost while trying to trace through how all this works in ActiveRecord, so I haven't been able to pinpoint the error.\r\n\r\n### Expected behavior\r\n\r\nThe example testcase should pass. The write connection should not end up marked as readonly.\r\n\r\n### Actual behavior\r\n\r\nTestcase fails with\r\n\r\n```\r\nActiveRecord::ReadOnlyError: Write query attempted while in readonly mode: INSERT INTO \"users\" (\"name\", \"created_at\", \"updated_at\") VALUES (?, ?, ?)\r\n    test/controllers/site_controller_test.rb:13:in `test_index'\r\n```\r\n\r\n### System configuration\r\n**Rails version**: 6.0.3\r\n\r\n**Ruby version**: ruby 2.6.5p114\r\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/issues/39449/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/issues/39449/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":"completed"}},"event":"cross-referenced"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/issues/comments/679259084","html_url":"https://github.com/rails/rails/issues/39205#issuecomment-679259084","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rails/rails/issues/39205","id":679259084,"node_id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY3OTI1OTA4NA==","user":{"login":"jji-fieldwire","id":57971167,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU3OTcxMTY3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/57971167?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jji-fieldwire","html_url":"https://github.com/jji-fieldwire","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jji-fieldwire/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jji-fieldwire/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jji-fieldwire/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jji-fieldwire/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jji-fieldwire/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jji-fieldwire/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jji-fieldwire/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jji-fieldwire/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jji-fieldwire/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2020-08-24T17:20:40Z","updated_at":"2020-08-24T17:20:40Z"